{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And her father spit in her face.</b> If her father were angry at her and spat in her face, would she not be ashamed to see his face for seven days? — all the more so (קל וחומר) one who spoke and made light of the awe of the Shekhinah, showing contempt for His prophet, that she deserves to be punished and to be shamed for fourteen days; but ״it is enough for that which comes from the inference to be as the subject inferred from,״ as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded. They called the Shekhinah ״that which comes from the inference,״ and they said ״it is enough,״ meaning He does not want more than the law of her father, which is the subject inferred from. According to this, it appears that the spitting is a metaphor for anger and shame, not for the sin; for if we say that it is a metaphor for the sin, it should have said 'and if her father spat in her face,' since Miriam was the one who sinned, and the metaphor of the spitting comes to illustrate the sin, because the sin she committed was the repulsive speech alluded to in the word נואלנו, and therefore they likened it to spitting, which is a repulsive thing — like the matter of spitting in the mystery of the commandment of levirate marriage. And if we force the interpretation and say that when it says 'and her father spat in her face,' it is a euphemism for the honor of her father and for her own honor, it is impossible to say so, for then it should have said 'that he would be ashamed,' since the shame does not fall upon the one who dishonors but upon the one who received the dishonor." "<b>But</b> the correct view here is that the spitting is a metaphor for anger and shame, and because the Holy One, blessed be He, had shamed her with leprosy, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: And how can you say that I should heal her? If her father spat in her face — meaning, He had shamed her because of her sin against Him — would she not be ashamed for seven days? All the more so on My account, since I shamed her and struck her with leprosy for what she sinned and for what she spoke against you and came against Me; therefore she shall be shut up seven days and afterward be gathered. And for this reason He took up the metaphor of the spitting by which her father would shame her, because that is the sin of leprosy, through which she shamed her Father on high, blessed be He. And this matter of Miriam is called by our Rabbis, of blessed memory, a נזיפה (reproof), and there are three kinds of reproof: there is a reproof of one day, and there is a reproof of seven, and there is one of thirty days. One who disparages a Torah scholar by speaking about him or doing something to pain him and vex him, and he knows that that Torah scholar holds it against him in his heart, must observe a reproof of one day, and he does not need a formal release afterward, since this matter is only to appease the mind of the Torah scholar; and this one day must be a complete whole day — we do not say in it 'a partial day counts as a full day.' One who speaks against a prophet or against one of the great sages must observe a reproof of seven days, and we learn this from Miriam, who spoke against a prophet, and the Torah required her to observe a reproof of seven, and we do not say in these seven days 'a partial day counts as a full day.' One who speaks against a nasi (prince/head) must observe a reproof of thirty days, and we do not say in it 'a partial day counts as a full day'; and your mnemonic for these three types of reproof is האבן האז״ל. And they said in the Yerushalmi: there is no reproof less than seven, as it says, ״she shall be shut up seven days״; and there is no ban of excommunication (נדוי) less than thirty days, as it says (Numbers 11:20) ״עד חדש ימים עד אשר יצא מאפכם״." "<b>And the fact</b> that he began with ואביה — the ו is at the start of an utterance — this is the way of Scripture in many places; and like it (Numbers 18:8): ״וידבר ה׳ אל אהרן ואני הנה נתתי לך״; and likewise (Genesis 27:28): ״ויתן לך האלהים״; and also (Numbers 9:2): ״ויעשו בני ישראל את הפסח״; and likewise (II Kings 2:9): ״ויאמר אלישע ויהי נא פי שנים ברוחך אלי״; and there is no doubt that each and every one has a precise reason, if we could grasp it." "<b>And because</b> the principle of קל וחומר (inference from minor to major) is one of the thirteen principles by which the Torah is expounded, and it is the first of them all, I have seen fit to set them out here as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, explained them. This is what we learned in the Baraita: Rabbi Yishmael said — the Torah is expounded by thirteen principles. <b>The first: קל וחומר (inference from minor to major),</b> and they derived it from this verse, ״and her father spat in her face,״ and also from what is written (Exodus 23:4): ״כי תראה חמור שונאך״ — from the hated one we infer a קל וחומר to the beloved; this is the first principle. <b>The second: גזרה שוה (verbal analogy / equal decree),</b> which is what is written (Leviticus 18:10): ״ערות בת בנך או בת בתך לא תגלה ערותן כי ערותך הנה״ — and it forbade only the daughter, and another verse says (Leviticus 18:17): ״ערות אשה ובתה לא תגלה את בת בנה ואת בת בתה לא תקרב לגלות ערותה שארה הנה זמה היא,״ and we learn a גזרה שוה from הנה–הנה: just as there it is a woman and her daughter and her son's daughter and her daughter's daughter, so too here it is his daughter and his son's daughter and his daughter's daughter. <b>The third: מבנין אב וכתוב אחד (construction of a general principle from a single verse),</b> which is what is written regarding the festival of unleavened bread (Exodus 12:16–17): ״אך אשר יאכל לכל נפש הוא לבדו יעשה לכם״ — the verse permitted us only food for the soul (i.e., food preparation) on the festival of unleavened bread, and on the other appointed times it did not explicitly permit food for the soul; therefore our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: this is a בנין אב for all the appointed times. Also from what is written (Leviticus 20:9): ״דמיו בו,״ and they said, of blessed memory: wherever it says ״דמיו בו״ or ״דמיהם בם,״ it means nothing but stoning, as it is written concerning the ov and yidoni (Leviticus 20:27): ״באבן ירגמו אותם דמיהם בם״ — it is nothing but stoning; this is a בנין אב from a single verse. A בנין אב from two verses: that is what is written concerning the blemishes of a man (Leviticus 21:20): ״או גבן או דק או תבלול בעינו או גרב או ילפת או מרוח אשך,״ and it did not mention יבלת (wart); but concerning the blemishes of an animal it did mention יבלת, as it says (Leviticus 22:22): ״עורת או שבור או חרוץ או יבלת״; and they said, of blessed memory: wherever a blemish is found stated indeterminately in the Torah, we apply to it both the blemishes of a man and the blemishes of an animal; this is a בנין אב from two verses. <b>The fourth: כלל ופרט (general and particular),</b> which is what is written (Deuteronomy 22:11): ״לא תלבש שעטנז,״ which is of two kinds, and afterward it specified for us ״צמר ופשתים יחדו״ — wool and linen together; פשתים (linen), yes; other materials, no. And wherever a general and particular are found in the Torah, the general includes only what is in the particular. <b>The fifth: פרט וכלל (particular and general),</b> which is what is written concerning returning a lost article (Deuteronomy 22:3–4): ״וכן תעשה לחמורו וכן תעשה לשמלתו,״ and what is understood from this is that these you are obligated to return, but not other things; and afterward it generalizes: ״וכן תעשה לכל אבדת אחיך.״ In every place where a particular and general are found in the Torah, you follow the general. <b>The sixth: כלל ופרט וכלל אי אתה דן אלא כעין הפרט (general and particular and general — you infer only according to the category of the particular),</b> which is what is written (Exodus 22:8): ״על כל דבר פשע״ — a general, for every matter of trespass in the world; and afterward it specifies ״על שור על חמור״ and so on; and afterward it generalizes ״על כל אבדה״; therefore you infer only according to the category of the particular: just as the particular is explicitly something movable whose body is monetary value, so too everything movable whose body is monetary value — excluding land, which is not movable; excluding slaves, which are equated to land, as it is written (Leviticus 25:46): ״והתנחלתם אותם לבניכם אחריכם לרשת אחזה לעולם בהם תעבדו״; excluding documents, for even though they are movable, their body is not monetary value. Also from what is written (Deuteronomy 14:26): ״ונתת הכסף בכל אשר תאוה נפשך״ — a general; ״בבקר ובצאן וביין ובשכר״ — a particular; ״ובכל אשר תשאלך נפשך״ — it returned and generalized — a general and particular and general: you infer only according to the category of the particular; just as the particular is explicitly produce and growths of the soil, so too everything that is produce and growths of the soil. <b>The seventh: the general that requires a particular,</b> which is what is written (Numbers 3:40): ״פקוד כל בכור זכר,״ if it had said זכר and not said בכור, I would have said every male, whether firstborn or not firstborn; therefore it says בכור, and this is a general that requires a particular. <b>The eighth: anything that was in the general and departed from the general — if it departed to teach, it did not depart to teach about itself, but departed to teach about the entire general,</b> which is what is written concerning Molech (Leviticus 20:2): ״איש איש מבני ישראל ומן הגר הגר בישראל אשר יתן מזרעו למולך מות יומת,״ and Molech was included among the other abominations; and why did it specify its punishment more than all of them? Not to teach about itself — that it is punishable by stoning — but to teach about the rest of the abominations, that they too are punishable by stoning. <b>The ninth: anything that was in the general and departed to be subject to a new ruling not like its own case — it departed both to lighten and to stringency,</b> which is what is written (Exodus 21:2): ״כי תקנה עבד עברי״ and so on; and it took out the Hebrew maidservant from the general, as it is written (Exodus 21:7): ״וכי ימכור איש את בתו לאמה,״ and surely the Hebrew maidservant was included in the general of the עבד עברי (Hebrew slave), as it is written (Deuteronomy 15:12): ״כי ימכר לך אחיך העברי או העבריה״ — why did she depart from the general? To be subject to a new ruling not like that of the slave: the slave does not go free by signs (of bodily blemishes) and does not go free by the death of the master even within the six years; she departed to lighten, for the matter was lightened for her that she goes free within six, and to stringency, that her master may betroth her against her will. <b>The tenth: anything that was in the general and departed to be subject to one ruling that is like its own case — it departed to lighten and not to stringency,</b> which is what is written (Deuteronomy 19:5): ״ואשר יבא את רעהו ביער לחטוב עצים״ and so on; one who strikes a soul unintentionally was included in the general category of murderer, as it is written (Leviticus 24:17): ״ומכה אדם יומת,״ whether unintentional or intentional; and one who strikes unintentionally departed from the general to be subject to one ruling that is like the case of one who strikes intentionally — that this one is killed and that one is killed; and it departed from the general to teach about itself that he is saved in the cities of refuge; this is ״and not to stringency.״ <b>The eleventh: anything that was in the general and departed to be judged by a new matter — you cannot return it to its general until the Scripture returns it to its general explicitly,</b> which is what is written (Leviticus 22:11): ״וכהן כי יקנה נפש קנין כספו הוא יאכל בו ויליד ביתו״ and so on; and his daughter was included in יליד ביתו, whether unmarried or married; and she departed to be judged by a new matter: after she married she does not eat of the terumah, as it is written (Leviticus 22:12): ״ובת כהן כי תהיה לאיש זר היא בתרומת הקדשים לא תאכל;״ since the Scripture took her out of the general, you cannot return her to her general even as a widow or divorcée unless the Scripture returns her to her general explicitly, as it is written (Leviticus 22:13): ״ובת כהן כי תהיה אלמנה וגרושה וזרע אין לה ושבה אל בית אביה״ and so on. <b>The twelfth: a matter learned from its context and a matter learned from its conclusion,</b> which is what is written in the matter of the Sabbath (Exodus 16:29): ״שבו איש תחתיו,״ and one should not say that they must sit in their places the entire day, but rather that no man shall leave his place two thousand cubits — this is a matter learned from its context. But a matter learned from its conclusion is what is written in the matter of forbidden relations (Leviticus 18:6): ״איש איש אל כל שאר בשרו לא תקרבו לגלות ערוה״ — it forbade you all kinship in the world, and at the end of the entire passage it specified all those who are forbidden, as it said ״ערות פלוני ופלוני לא תגלה,״ and from them we can learn those who are permitted — this is a matter learned from its conclusion. <b>The thirteenth: two verses that contradict each other, until a third verse comes and decides between them,</b> which is what is written (Genesis 1:1): ״בראשית ברא אלהים את השמים ואת הארץ,״ and from this it appears that the heavens were created first; and it is written (Genesis 2:4): ״ארץ ושמים,״ from which it appears that the earth was created first — the third verse came and decided between them, to say that there is no contradiction, as it is written (Isaiah 48:13): ״אף ידי יסדה ארץ וימיני טפחה שמים קורא אני אליהם יעמדו יחדו.״ And likewise (Exodus 20:19): ״אתם ראיתם כי מן השמים דברתי עמכם,״ and from this it appears that He did not speak to them on the mountain; and it is written in the Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 5:4): ״פנים בפנים דבר ה׳ עמכם בהר מתוך האש״ — the third verse came and decided between them, to say that there is no contradiction, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:36): ״מן השמים השמיעך את קולו ליסרך ועל הארץ הראך את אשו הגדולה.״ And likewise (Leviticus 3:17): ״כל חלב וכל דם לא תאכלו,״ and from this it appears whether that of a wild animal or that of a domesticated animal; and it is written (Deuteronomy 12:22): ״כאשר יאכל את הצבי ואת האיל״ and so on — the third verse came and decided between them (Leviticus 7:23): ״כל חלב שור וכשב ועז לא תאכלו.״"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:46.250759600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}